      {
        "number": 8,
        "internalPageId": null,
        "title": "DEFINITIONS & POSSIBLE CASE INDEPENDENT CLAUSE DEPENDENT CLAUSE 1 They can exist alone I would pay a private college (IF I had the money.) 2 They need another clause to exist (She could call Mark) IF she wanted to. 3 Switching Clauses If they flunked the test , Their parents would ground them for life. To Consider 1. The use of would/could is commonly seen here to express the possibility about something. 2. The dependent clauses are used in simple past tense. 3. When the IF clause is the first one, it needs to be separated by a comma (,) from the other clause. 4. For the verb BE, the correct form to use is: If I were...",
        "visibleTexts": [
          "DEFINITIONS & POSSIBLE CASE INDEPENDENT CLAUSE DEPENDENT CLAUSE 1 They can exist alone I would pay a private college (IF I had the money.) 2 They need another clause to exist (She could call Mark) IF she wanted to. 3 Switching Clauses If they flunked the test , Their parents would ground them for life. To Consider 1. The use of would/could is commonly seen here to express the possibility about something. 2. The dependent clauses are used in simple past tense. 3. When the IF clause is the first one, it needs to be separated by a comma (,) from the other clause. 4. For the verb BE, the correct form to use is: If I were...",
          "Conditionals link two different clauses as we learned in the last unit. Second conditional links a possibility with a situation unlikely to happen."
        ],
